  "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}+12y-18x & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2}+12y-9{x}^{2}-18x & =9 \\\\ [{y}^{2}+12y]-9[{x}^{2}+2x] & =9 \\\\ [(y+6)^{2}-36]-9[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =9 \\\\ (y+6)^{2}-36-9(x+1)^{2}+9 & =9 \\\\ (y+6)^{2}-9(x+1)^{2} & =9+36-9 \\\\ (y+6)^{2}-9(x+1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-(-6))^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(\\,-1,-6\\,).$",
